    "text": "## **2.4 Voronoi partition and data clustering** \n\nHere we introduce some preliminaries on Voronoi partition that we will use in the rest of this paper. Given a set of seeds (centres) _C_ = � _c_<sup>(1)</sup> _, · · · , c_<sup>( ˆ</sup> _K_ )<sup>�</sup> , _c_<sup>(</sup><sup>_i_)</sup> _∈_ R<sup>_d_</sup> , a Voronoi partition _V_ ( _C_ ) partitions the R<sup>_d_</sup> space to _K_<sup>ˆ</sup> cells _V_<sup>(1)</sup> _, · · · , V_<sup>( ˆ</sup> _K_ ) such that any point in _V_ ( _j_ ), _∀j ∈_ N[1 _,_ ˆ _K_ ]<sup>,iscloserto</sup><sup>_c_(</sup><sup>_j_)thantheother</sup> seeds. Given a set of points _P_ = � _p_<sup>(1)</sup> _, · · · , p_<sup>(</sup><sup>_K_)�</sup> in R<sup>_d_</sup> , we use _VP_ ( _C_ ) to show the partition of _P_ through a Voronoi partition with seeds _C_ . We define each cell (may also be referred to as partition in this paper) of _VP_ ( _C_ ) as \n\n\n\nwhere _d_ ( _p_<sup>(1)</sup> _, p_<sup>(2)</sup> ) is the distance of _p_<sup>(1)</sup> _∈_ R<sup>_d_</sup> from _p_<sup>(2)</sup> _∈_ R<sup>_d_</sup> in any valid metric (Euclidean norm is used in this paper). We denote the number of elements in _V_<sup>(</sup><sup>_j_)</sup> by _|V_<sup>(</sup><sup>_j_)</sup> _|_ . \n\nA given set _P ∈X_<sup>_K_</sup> with _K_ points in R<sup>_d_</sup> can be clustered in _K_<sup>ˆ</sup> clusters by finding a set of seeds _C ∈X K_<sup>ˆ</sup> that minimizes the _within-cluster sum of squares_ , as proposed by _k_ -means method/Lloyd’s algorithm [20]. The within-cluster sum of squares, denoted by WSS( _K,_<sup>ˆ</sup> _VP_ ( _C_ )), simply represents the total sum of squared deviations of points in cells from their seeds, i.e., given a set of points _P_ , a set of seeds _C_ , and a partition set _VP_ ( _C_ ) = _{V_<sup>(1)</sup> _, · · · , V_<sup>( ˆ</sup> _K_ ) _}_ , \n\n\n\n\n\nbe the set of optimal seeds. Given _C_<sup>_∗_</sup> , cells of _VP_ ( _C_<sup>_∗_</sup> ) represent the optimal _K_<sup>ˆ</sup> clusters of _P_ . Although solving (11) is an NP-hard problem [21], efficient algorithms exist to compute a local minima. Starting from an initial guess of _C_ , a successive algorithm with time complexity _O_ ( _ndKK_<sup>ˆ</sup> ) for _n_ iterations can be used to update each seed by replacing it with the centroid of its cluster elements [22]. This process continues until convergence or _n_ reaches its maximum value. Note that the set of optimal seeds _C_<sup>_∗_</sup> is not necessarily a subset of _P_ . In addition, WSS( _K,_<sup>ˆ</sup> _VP_ ( _C_ )) is non-increasing in _K_<sup>ˆ</sup> , and the number of required clusters can be decided by making a trade-off between WSS( _K,_<sup>ˆ</sup> _VP_ ( _C_ )) (representing accuracy) and _K_<sup>ˆ</sup> (representing computational complexity). \n\n**Lemma 1.** _Seed selection and Voronoi configuration are preserved under translation._ \n\n_1. Let C_<sup>_∗_</sup> = _{c_<sup>(1)</sup> _, · · · , c_<sup>( ˆ</sup> _K_ ) _} be the set of K_ ˆ _optimal seeds of P_ = _{p_ (1) _, · · · , p_ ( _K_ ) _}, calculated as in_ (11) _. For any fixed vector a, Ca_<sup>_∗_=</sup><sup>_{a_+</sup><sup>_c_(1)</sup><sup>_, · · ·, a_+</sup><sup>_c_( ˆ</sup> _K_ ) _} represents the optimal seeds of Pa_ = _{a_ + _p_<sup>(1)</sup> _, · · · , a_ + _p_<sup>(</sup><sup>_K_)</sup> _}._ \n\n\n\n_Proof:_ The proof is straight forward since both WSS and Voronoi partitions (as given in (10) and (9)) are functions of the relative distance of the points in each cell to their seed. \n\n# **3 Scenarios required to meet given failure tolerance** \n\nGiven i.i.d. _y_<sup>(1)</sup> _, y_<sup>(2)</sup> _, . . . , y_<sup>(</sup><sup>_K_)</sup> for _K >_ 0 and _y_<sup>(</sup><sup>_i_)</sup> _∈_ [0 _,_ 1] _, ∀i ∈_ N[1 _,K_ ] with probability measure P _y_ , we define the concatenation of these random variables _Y_ = [ _y_<sup>(1)</sup> _y_<sup>(2)</sup> _. . . y_<sup>(</sup><sup>_K_)</sup> ]<sup>_⊤_</sup> _∈_ [0 _,_ 1]<sup>_K_</sup> . The probability measure associated with _Y_ is P<sup>_K_</sup